## Why This Is Asked

Tail-latency across CNPA is a common production challenge. This question probes practical, end-to-end observability skills across multiple components, including tracing, instrumentation, sampling, and validation.

## Key Concepts

- Distributed tracing with OpenTelemetry
- Context propagation across HTTP, PostgreSQL, Kafka, and Redis
- End-to-end latency analysis with tail metrics (p95/p99)
- Sampling strategies and dashboards/alerts
- Validation via canary or small controlled rollout

## Code Example

```javascript
// OpenTelemetry trace propagation across CNPA (illustrative)
import { trace, propagation, context, SpanKind } from '@opentelemetry/api';
const tracer = trace.getTracer('cnpa');
const span = tracer.startSpan('handleRequest', { kind: SpanKind.SERVER });
// Inject trace context into downstream calls
const reqHeaders = {};
propagation.inject(context.active(), reqHeaders, {
  set: (carrier, key, value) => { carrier[key] = value; }
});
// ... downstream calls carry reqHeaders
span.end();
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adjust sampling to balance insight and overhead at 1000 RPS vs 100k RPS?
- How would you validate that tracing changes do not introduce measurable latency overhead?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to reason about idempotency and delivery guarantees in CNPA patterns, at a beginner level, while nudging toward practical implementation details.

## Key Concepts

- Idempotent enrichment
- Exactly-once delivery
- Outbox pattern
- Deduplication cache
- Producer config (idempotence, acks)

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: dedup check before processing
async function enrich(event, db, cache) {
  const id = event.event_id;
  if (await cache.has(id)) return; // already processed
  const profile = await db.getProfile(event.user_id);
  const enriched = { ...event, profile };
  await cache.add(id, /* ttl */ 60000);
  await publishToKafka(enriched);
  await writeOutbox(enriched);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test edge cases like cache eviction and outbox failures?
- How would you adapt if events arrive out of order or with duplicate IDs?

## Why This Is Asked
New per-user ordering constraint across partitions requires understanding coupling points and strong guarantees.

## Key Concepts
- Per-user partitioning and outbox pattern
- Kafka transactions, idempotence, max.in.flight
- Idempotent sinks and strict ordering verification

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: write to outbox and commit Kafka txn atomically
await db.transaction(async trx => {
  await trx.into('outbox').insert({user_id, event, seq});
  await kafkaProducer.beginTransaction();
  await kafkaProducer.send({topic, messages:[{key: String(user_id), value: event}]});
  await kafkaProducer.commitTransaction();
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you monitor and alert on per-user ordering violations?
- How would you handle a hot user skew if a single user dominates partition leadership?

